# Linear: triage and tracking work

First check `.harness/local/skills/linear-triage.md`: if the file exists, read it before starting work. It is the project addendum — who is who in the workspace, how to verify a rollout, the project's own labels and triage rules; where it disagrees with the text below, the addendum wins.

Linear plays two roles. The first is the channel with the customer: they may not be technical, they write issues and remarks in their own words and read the answers there. The second is the journal of all work: every change, fix, and harness PR is an issue in the stage's project. Linear is not the plan: the plan lives in `openspec/changes/<name>/tasks.md`, requirements in the spec (`{{ spec_path }}`), open questions in `{{ open_questions_path }}`.

Linear is accessed through its MCP server.

## Tracking work

- **One issue per change, fix, or harness PR.** Created before the first push: at propose, at the start of a fix. Project — the current stage (stage table `{{ stages }}`), assignee — the owner, labels — one from the `Type` group plus areas. Description: change name, spec paragraph, what changes. Items of `tasks.md` don't become issues.
- **`{{ tracker_prefix }}-N` at the start of the PR title** ("{{ tracker_prefix }}-34 <package>: …") and in the body. That is how Linear links the PR to the issue.
- **Work from a customer issue** is tracked in that issue; no new one is created.
- **An executor in a parallel session** gets the number from the curator and doesn't create the issue itself.

Triage is done by the main session and is not handed to a subagent: every issue is a decision about where it goes.

## Who is who

The owner decides requirements and edits the spec. The customer is the one the product is built for and from whom issues and remarks come. Who is who in the workspace is recorded in the skill addendum (`.harness/local/skills/linear-triage.md`) or in `AGENTS.md`; if it isn't and there are more than two members, ask the owner rather than guess.

## Untriaged issue

Untriaged is an issue that has no project and is not in Canceled. A triaged issue always has a project: the current stage or the next one. Query — issues without a project filter, then drop the ones with a project.

## Triaging one issue

1. **Understand what is asked.** Unclear — a comment to the customer with a question. The issue stays without a project until there is an answer.
2. **Name the kind**; the route depends on it:

| Kind | Sign | Route |
|---|---|---|
| Bug | The system does something other than what is already written in the spec or `openspec/specs/` | `Bug`. Handled by a stage change or a fix; we decide ourselves |
| Request | Something not in the spec is needed: a new metric, screen, rule | `Feature` and a question to the owner. There are no requirements without the spec: until the owner adds the request to the spec, no change is created from it |
| "How does this work" question | The customer asks rather than requests | Answer in a comment, issue — Done |
| Answer to our question | The customer wrote an issue instead of a comment | Move the answer to the issue with the question, this one — Duplicate |

3. **File it.** The stage project, one label from the `Type` group (`Bug`, `Feature`, `Improvement`), area labels (by project packages, and `harness` for the harness), and `Customer` for customer issues. Labels are in English. Process labels — `Customer`, `Question for customer`, `Awaiting deploy`.
4. **Reply to the customer in a comment**: what we understood and what's next — "taken", "asked the owner", "it already works this way, here is where to look".

## How to write to the customer

In their words. From the spec, only the words of the glossary section. Internal code names, states, paragraph numbers, change and file names — not allowed. An example and answer options are better than abstraction. If the answer depends on how the customer uses the product — ask them. If it depends on how we store things and when we do them — ask the owner.

## Our question to the customer

An issue in the current stage's project. Title — the question itself, description — text for the customer with an example, label `Question for customer`, assignee — the customer. In `{{ open_questions_path }}` the question has a line `Linear: {{ tracker_prefix }}-N`. When the answer arrives, the question is closed with the `knowledge-note` skill quoting the answer, and the issue goes to Done.

## Further along the issue's life

| Event | In Linear |
|---|---|
| Change created or fix started | In Progress, change name in the description |
| PR opened | In Review, link to the PR |
| PR merged | label `Awaiting deploy` |
| Rolled out | Done, remove `Awaiting deploy`; on a customer issue — a comment to them: what changed and how to check it, in their words |
| Change abandoned | Canceled with the reason |

Work without a rollout (spec, `knowledge/`, harness) doesn't wait for one: Done on merge. How to check that the merge commit is rolled out is in the skill addendum; if there is none, ask the owner and offer to record the answer there. Every triage also checks this column: issues with `Awaiting deploy` are closed as soon as their commit is rolled out.

Only we move statuses: Linear's Git automation is kept off, and the `{{ tracker_prefix }}-N` number in a PR or branch only adds a link to the issue. If a status changed by itself, the automation was turned on. Return the issue to its previous status and tell the owner.

## Report to the owner

Three lists: what was filed and where, what awaits their decision (requests outside the spec), what went to the customer as a question. An empty triage is one line.

## What not to do

- Don't create a change from a request that isn't in the spec, and don't add to the spec yourself.
- Don't move `tasks.md` items or questions for the owner into Linear: questions for the owner are asked in chat.
- Don't push a change or fix without an issue: work that isn't in Linear doesn't exist for the owner.
- Don't set Done on merging code that isn't rolled out yet: the change is visible only after rollout.
- Don't leave a customer issue without an answer: a triaged issue always carries a comment.
